course: books
lesson: 21
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter XI, part 1: Who Stole the Tarts? (the court and the first witness)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Alice arrives at a court of justice. The King and Queen of Hearts sit on their throne, the Knave of Hearts stands in chains between two soldiers, and the White Rabbit holds a trumpet and a scroll. In the middle is a dish of tarts that look so good that Alice wishes they'd hurry up and hand round the refreshments.
explain: Alice is pleased to know the names of things from books: the judge is the King, because of his great wig (with his crown on top, which looks most uncomfortable), and the twelve creatures in the jury-box are the jurors. She is proud of that word. The jurors are writing down their own names, in case they forget them before the end.
explain: When Alice says «Stupid things!», the jurors all write «stupid things!» on their slates, and one of them can't spell «stupid». Another juror's pencil squeaks; Alice sneaks behind him and takes it, and poor Bill the Lizard has to write with his finger, which leaves no mark at all.
explain: The White Rabbit blows three blasts on the trumpet and reads the accusation as a nursery rhyme: the Queen made some tarts, and the Knave stole them. The King says «Consider your verdict» at once, and the Rabbit has to stop him: there's a great deal to come first!
explain: The first witness is the Hatter, still holding his teacup and bread-and-butter. He, the March Hare and the Dormouse each give a different date for when the tea began, and the jury adds them all up as money. The King tells him to take off his hat, cries «Stolen!» when the Hatter says it isn't his, and then warns: «don't be nervous, or I'll have you executed on the spot». The Hatter is so nervous he bites a piece out of his teacup.
fact: The court: the King and Queen on their throne, birds, beasts and the whole pack of cards; the Knave «in chains, with a soldier on each side»; the White Rabbit with «a trumpet in one hand, and a scroll of parchment in the other».
fact: In the middle of the court is «a table, with a large dish of tarts upon it» (tarts = small pastries with jam or fruit).
fact: Alice had never been in a court of justice, but had read about them in books; she knows the judge «because of his great wig» — and the judge is the King, wearing his crown over his wig.
fact: There are twelve jurors, some animals and some birds, so Alice calls them «creatures»; she is proud of knowing the word «jurors», because she thinks very few little girls of her age know what it means, though «jury-men» would have done as well.
fact: A jury is a group of ordinary people who listen to a trial and decide if the accused is guilty; their decision is the «verdict».
fact: The jurors write their names on slates «for fear they should forget them before the end of the trial» (a slate was a small board children wrote on with a slate-pencil).
fact: Alice says «Stupid things!»; the jurors all write it down, and one of them «didn't know how to spell "stupid"» and had to ask his neighbour.
fact: Bill the Lizard's pencil squeaks, which Alice «could not stand»; she takes it away, and he has to write with one finger, which «left no mark on the slate».
fact: The White Rabbit is the herald, the officer who reads announcements; he blows three blasts on the trumpet before reading the accusation, and three more before calling out «First witness!»
fact: The accusation: «The Queen of Hearts, she made some tarts, All on a summer day: The Knave of Hearts, he stole those tarts, And took them quite away!» — a real old nursery rhyme.
fact: «Consider your verdict,» the King says at once; «Not yet, not yet!» says the Rabbit: the evidence must come first.
fact: The Hatter says tea began on the «Fourteenth of March»; the March Hare says «Fifteenth», the Dormouse «Sixteenth»; the jury adds them up and turns the answer into shillings and pence (old British money).
fact: «Take off your hat.» — «It isn't mine.» — «Stolen!» — «I keep them to sell... I'm a hatter.» A hatter is a person who makes or sells hats.
fact: «Give your evidence, and don't be nervous, or I'll have you executed on the spot» — the King's threat makes the Hatter even more nervous, and he bites a piece out of his teacup; the Queen puts on her spectacles and stares hard at the Hatter, the singer who «murdered the time» at her concert.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «bare» (London) = lots of; «peng» (London) = delicious, great-looking; «nesh» (North and Midlands) = feeling the cold easily; «wicked» (New England) = very; «do the needful» (Indian English) = do what must be done.
exercise: Who sits on the throne when Alice arrives at the court? | The King and Queen of Hearts | The Hatter and the March Hare | The Duchess and her cook | The Gryphon and the Mock Turtle, side by side on a rock
exercise: Who stands before the throne in chains? | The Knave of Hearts | The Hatter | The Duchess, back from prison | Bill the Lizard
exercise: What does the White Rabbit hold? | A trumpet and a scroll of parchment | A fan and a pair of white kid gloves | A teapot | A flamingo
exercise: What is on the table in the middle of the court? | A large dish of tarts | A teapot and cups from the March Hare's table | A pepper-box | The golden key
exercise: What does Alice wish, looking at the tarts? | That they'd get the trial done and hand round the refreshments | That the Knave would steal another one for her, quickly | That the tarts were treacle | That she had never seen them
exercise: How does Alice know who the judge is? | Because of his great wig | Because he holds a hammer | Because he sits highest of all | Because the Rabbit tells her
exercise: Who is the judge? | The King | The Queen | The White Rabbit | The Gryphon
exercise: How many jurors are there? | Twelve | About ten | Only three | Twenty-one
exercise: Why does Alice call the jurors «creatures»? | Some of them were animals, and some were birds | They were very strange to look at, like the Mock Turtle | The Queen calls them that | She forgot the word
exercise: Why is Alice proud of the word «jurors»? | She thinks very few little girls her age know what it means | It is a French word she learned from her lesson-book, just after «Où est ma chatte?» | It is the longest word she knows | The Gryphon taught her it
exercise: What are the jurors writing before the trial begins? | Their own names, for fear they should forget them | The names of all the tarts on the dish, one by one, in a list | The accusation | A song
exercise: What do the jurors write when Alice says «Stupid things!»? | «Stupid things!» | «Silence in the court!» | Alice's name in full | Nothing at all on their slates
exercise: What is the problem with one juror and the word «stupid»? | He doesn't know how to spell it | He can't read it | He thinks it means clever and writes «clever things» instead | He writes it in French
exercise: Whose pencil squeaks? | Bill the Lizard's | The sleepy Dormouse's | The White Rabbit's | The King of Hearts'
exercise: What does Bill do after Alice takes his pencil? | Writes with one finger, which leaves no mark | Borrows the Queen's pen and a pot of red ink | Cries | Leaves the court
exercise: What does the White Rabbit do before reading the accusation? | Blows three blasts on the trumpet | Bows low to the Queen three times and kisses her hand | Rings a bell | Puts on his gloves
exercise: In the accusation, who made the tarts? | The Queen of Hearts | The Duchess's old cook | The Knave of Hearts himself | The March Hare, at tea
exercise: In the accusation, what did the Knave do? | Stole the tarts and took them quite away | Ate all the tarts on a summer day, sitting by the river | Made the tarts | Gave the tarts to Alice
exercise: What does the King say straight after the accusation? | «Consider your verdict» | «Off with his head» | «Call Alice» | «Read it again»
exercise: Why does the White Rabbit say «Not yet, not yet!»? | There's a great deal to come before the verdict | The tarts aren't ready | The Queen is fast asleep on the throne | The jury can't write
exercise: Who is the first witness? | The Hatter | The March Hare | The Dormouse | The Duchess
exercise: What does the Hatter bring into court? | A teacup and a piece of bread-and-butter | His watch and a pot of the best butter | A big hat-box full of hats to sell | The Dormouse
exercise: What dates do the Hatter, the March Hare and the Dormouse give? | The fourteenth, fifteenth and sixteenth of March | The first, second and third of May, one after another | The fourth of every month | Six o'clock
exercise: What does the jury do with the three dates? | Adds them up and turns the answer into shillings and pence | Writes down only the first one, the Hatter's, and rubs out the others | Throws them away | Sings them
exercise: «Take off your hat.» «It isn't mine.» What does the King conclude? | That it is stolen | That it is the Queen's | That it is a birthday gift | That it is a crown
exercise: Why isn't the hat the Hatter's own? | He keeps hats to sell; he has none of his own | He borrowed it from the March Hare for the trial | He found it in the garden | The Queen lent it
test: Abi: «the Queen was the judge, she had the wig on, innit.» (innit = isn't it) Put it right. | The judge was the King, wearing his crown over the wig | Right: the Queen wore a big wig and a crown, and banged a little hammer on the table | The White Rabbit was the judge | There was no judge
test: The jurors write their names «for fear they should forget them». What does this show? | They are very silly jurors | They are very careful and wise | They are still learning to write | They want each other's autographs
test: What would happen to Bill's evidence notes now that he writes with his finger? | Nothing would be on his slate at all | He would have the neatest notes in the court | He'd write in ink | He'd win a prize
test: Mason, kinda nesh (feeling the cold): «why'd Alice take the pencil, was she just being mean?» What's the reason in the book? | It squeaked, and she «could not stand» it | She wanted to draw the tarts | The Queen told her to | She needed it for her own slate, to write down all the evidence
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The King wants to hear all the evidence before the verdict.» | False: he says «Consider your verdict» straight away, and the Rabbit has to stop him | True: he insists on a long and careful trial, with every witness heard | True, but the Queen doesn't | False: there is no verdict in this book
test: Why does the jury adding up three dates as shillings and pence make no sense? | Dates are not money, and adding them gives nothing useful | Shillings were never used in England | They should be added as hours instead, which would give the right answer | It makes perfect sense
test: «Stolen!» the King cries about the hat. What has he got wrong? | The hat isn't the Hatter's because he sells hats, not because he stole it | He's right: the Hatter stole it from the Queen's palace when nobody was looking | The hat is a crown | The hat is the March Hare's
test: Priya, wicked excited: «the King told the Hatter, chill, don't be nervous, nobody gets hurt.» (wicked = very, New England) Put it right. | He said don't be nervous, «or I'll have you executed on the spot» | Right: he was very kind and patted the Hatter on the head and gave him a tart | He said nothing to the Hatter | He offered him tea
test: The King says «don't be nervous, or I'll have you executed». Why does this not help? | A threat makes anyone more nervous, not less | The Hatter is deaf | The Hatter is the King's friend | The King is only joking, and everyone in the court knows it
test: What shows how nervous the Hatter is? | He bites a large piece out of his teacup instead of the bread-and-butter | He sings «Twinkle, twinkle» | He takes off his hat | He hides behind the Dormouse and the March Hare
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, what happened to Bill the Lizard? | He was sent down the chimney, and Alice kicked him up «like a sky-rocket» | He fell into the pool of tears and swam ashore with the Dodo | He painted the roses | He sang at the concert
test: Looking back: the Hatter says tea began on the fourteenth of March. What else happened «last March», he told Alice? | He quarrelled with Time at the Queen's concert | The Dormouse fell asleep for good | He sold his very first hat to the King | He met Alice
test: Looking back: why does the Queen stare at the Hatter so hard, do you think? | He's the one who «murdered the time» singing at her concert | He owes her money for a hat he sold her last summer | He stole the tarts | He is her brother
test: Looking back: in chapter III, the Mouse's tale had a dog called Fury who said «I'll be judge, I'll be jury». What do the two trials have in common? | Both are unfair: one side controls the whole trial | Both are about stolen tarts | Both take place in the sea | Both have twelve jurors writing on slates
test: Looking back: in chapter VIII, what was the Knave of Hearts carrying in the procession? | The King's crown on a crimson velvet cushion | A dish of tarts | A trumpet | The Queen's flamingo, tucked under his arm
test: Looking back: in chapter I, Alice used the words «Latitude» and «Longitude» without knowing them. How is «jurors» different? | She does know what «jurors» means | She doesn't know what «jurors» means either | She can't say «jurors» | She learnt «jurors» in French
test: ★★★ Add the three dates the Hatter, March Hare and Dormouse give: 14 + 15 + 16. What do you get? | 45 | 30 | 46 | 44
test: ★★★ The White Rabbit blows three blasts before the accusation and three before calling the first witness. How many blasts in all so far? | 6 | 3 | 9 | 12
test: ★★★ Careful reading: who asks his neighbour how to spell «stupid»? | One of the jurors (the book doesn't say which) | Bill the Lizard, by name | The King | The White Rabbit
exam: Identify the means by which Alice ascertained the identity of the judge. | The presence of his great wig | The weight of his gavel and the gravity of his demeanour | The testimony of the Gryphon | The colour of his robes
exam: It is given that the jurors were engaged in writing prior to the commencement of the trial. Select the response which most accurately characterises the content of their writing. | Their own names, lest these be forgotten before the trial concluded | The text of the accusation | The verdict | The prices of the tarts
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the Hatter possessed no hat of his own. | He kept them for the purpose of sale, being a hatter | He had been deprived of it by the Queen | He had lost it at the croquet-ground during the game with the hedgehogs | Hats were forbidden in court
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the content of the accusation? | That the Queen made some tarts, which the Knave stole and took quite away | That the Hatter stole the tarts | That the Knave made the tarts | That the tarts were made of pepper by the Duchess's cook
